WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Well, tonight, a new debate is raging over the use of artificial intelligence in a film on the

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:12.340
life of celebrity chef Anthony Bourdain, who died by suicide in 2018. CBS's Jim Axelrod takes a look.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:17.080
The goal of the new documentary about Anthony Bourdain was straightforward.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.900
Here's a little preemptive truth telling.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.740
As the director, Morgan Neville, told me.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.540
I want people to be able to make some sense out of his death.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.120
But it turns out how it was made was not.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.780
You were successful, and I am successful, and I'm wondering, are you happy?

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.340
That soundbite was something Bourdain wrote, but never actually said.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:39.720
Listen again.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.300
You were successful, and I am successful.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:52.440
The voice was generated by a computer, a use of artificial intelligence Neville called a modern storytelling technique, but did not disclose to the viewer.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.480
I think it's hard to do that

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.840
because I think it's hard to

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.680
put a voice to something that

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:02.520
someone never said only wrote is

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.680
a really bad idea. I think to

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.520
do that and then not disclose

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.920
that you've done it is a

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.960
colossally bad idea. Syracuse

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.200
University Professor Robert

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.960
Thompson says using AI like

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.760
this only adds fuel to the fake

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.560
news fire. It's the harder I

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.540
that person really said that. Who knows if that really happened? Neville claims the AI voice was

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.980
used with the blessing of Bourdain's estate and literary agent. Though Bourdain's ex-wife tweeted,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.500
I certainly was not the one who said Tony would have been cool with that.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.840
The film opens today, but now with a computer-generated shadow hanging over it,

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.940
it's all too real. Jim Axelrod, CBS News.
